      "Language": "jpn",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "The polarized Th2 cells play an important role in the pathogenesis of atopic asthma as well as in the induction of airway inflammation. Th2 cytokines, such as IL-4, IL-5 and IL-13, are pivotal in regulating the allergic phenotype, the IgE response or the inflammatory cell-mediated function. Selective inhibition of Th2 cytokines by pharmacologic agents, including anti-cytokine blocking antibody, cytokine mutant and soluble cytokine receptor, will contribute to asthma therapy. Strategies based on blocking key signaling cytokines are also discussed."},
